'\" t
.\" man/man7/docv.7cr
.\" Generated by docv gen-cr from README.md and the doc comments; regenerate instead of editing.
.TH "docv" "7cr" "2026-11-03" "docv 0.1.0" "docv API Reference"
.SH "NAME"
docv \- Parses manual pages and Markdown documents and shows them in a terminal viewer.
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Parses manual pages and Markdown documents and shows them in a terminal viewer. Written in Crystal; besides the standard library it uses markd <https://github.com/icyleaf/markd> for Markdown and treesitter <https://github.com/shpeckman/treesitter> for syntax highlighting.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBParser.\fR A roff interpreter with the man(7) and mdoc(7) macro packages and the tbl(1) preprocessor built in natively. It reads plain and gzip page files and follows \fB\%.so\fR includes. Markdown files (CommonMark with the GitHub extensions) go through markd into the same model (see Markdown). The result is a document model of blocks and styled spans, with link targets and heading anchors.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLayout.\fR Turns a document into lines of cells. The \fIwrapped\fR layout fills paragraphs to the width. The \fIunwrapped\fR layout puts each paragraph on one line. Unfilled text and tables never wrap. Lines carry their links: the span links of a document, and cross\-references such as \fB\%ls(1)\fR detected in the text.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBViewer.\fR Uses a cell\-grid renderer that diffs the screen row by row. Keyboard input uses the kitty protocol, with all enhancements pushed and legacy encodings understood. Mouse input uses SGR\-pixel reports. The viewer also handles in\-band resize, focus, visibility and color\-scheme notifications. The screen is a title row (\fB\%\[uFF24]\[uFF2F]\[uFF23]\[uFF36]\fR and the version, which opens the help on a click, the page\(aqs heading centered, and a \[u1F7AA] close mark), a line, a dock on the left that toggles (\fB\%p\fR), the page with a draggable vertical scrollbar in the last column (only its thumb is drawn), a horizontal scrollbar line (thumb \fB\%\[u2501]\fR, track \fB\%\[u2500]\fR) and a status row. The heading of a manual page is its manual and reference (\fB\%ABRT \%Manual \%\- \%ABRT(1)\fR; pointing at it shows the footer\(aqs source and date beside it, dimmed), of a Markdown document its file name and first heading (\fB\%README.md \%\- \%docv\fR); the page itself scrolls under it, vertically and horizontally. The dock holds the search field and a tree of the current page\(aqs group (or of the current folder\(aqs pages and Markdown documents), names that share a prefix folded into nested groups and the current page selected. The field fuzzy\-finds in the displayed page, highlighting matches in place, while the tree counts the matching lines of each page; after a leading \fB\%/\fR the tree holds every page of the manual and ranks them instead. The mouse pointer takes the shape of what it is over (OSC 22). It also has links with back navigation (cross\-references, files, heading anchors; URLs as OSC 8 hyperlinks), mouse text selection copied to the clipboard, and a help (\fB\%h\fR, or a click on \fB\%\[uFF24]\[uFF2F]\[uFF23]\[uFF36]\fR) listing the keys as they are bound, shown in place of the page with \fB\%HELP\fR as the title row\(aqs heading. Everything is colored by role from the terminal\(aqs own theme, which it queries with OSC 21 (see Colors).
.SH
REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Crystal 1.21 or newer
.IP \(bu 2
Linux (the viewer uses \fB\%termios\fR and \fB\%TIOCGWINSZ\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
markd <https://github.com/icyleaf/markd> 0.6 and treesitter <https://github.com/shpeckman/treesitter> (installed by \fB\%shards \%install\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
libtree\-sitter 0.25 or newer, found through \fB\%pkg\-config\fR (Fedora: \fB\%libtree\-sitter\-devel\fR); it is linked in always
.IP \(bu 2
for syntax highlighting: tree\-sitter grammars and their \fB\%highlights.scm\fR queries (see Syntax highlighting)
.SH
INSTALLATION
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
dependencies:
  docv:
    github: shpeckman/docv
.EE
.RE
.PP
The command\-line viewer:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
shards install
make release        # bin/docv
make install        # \(ti/.local/bin/docv, man pages in \(ti/.local/share/man
.EE
.RE
.PP
The manual pages are \fB\%man/man1/docv.1\fR (the command, its keys, mouse, search path, environment and files) and \fB\%man/man5/docv\-config.5\fR (\fB\%config.ini\fR). Inside this repository \fB\%docv \%docv\fR finds them as project pages; after \fB\%make \%install\fR, \fB\%man \%docv\fR and \fB\%man \%docv\-config\fR work too.
.SH
COMMAND LINE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
docv [\-u]
docv [\-u] [section] name
docv [\-u] name(section)
docv [\-u] file
docv path\-add dir...
docv path\-rm dir...
docv path\-list
docv gen\-cr [\-o dir] [shard]
.EE
.RE
.PP
A \fB\%file\fR is a manual page, or a Markdown document when its name ends in \fB\%.md\fR, \fB\%.markdown\fR, \fB\%.mdown\fR, \fB\%.mkd\fR or \fB\%.mkdn\fR (optionally followed by \fB\%.gz\fR): \fB\%docv \%README.md\fR. Without a page, the viewer opens on an empty page with the dock closed and a hint in the status row; \fB\%p\fR opens the dock on the pages and documents of the current folder\(aqs project, \fB\%/\fR searches every page (this needs a terminal). The viewer starts in the wrapped layout unless \fB\%[viewer] \%start\-wrapped \%= \%false\fR is set in \fB\%config.ini\fR; \fB\%\-u\fR (\fB\%\-\-unwrapped\fR) starts in the unwrapped layout either way. When standard output is not a terminal, the page is printed as plain text at \fB\%MANWIDTH\fR columns (default 80).
.SS
Search path
.PP
Pages are looked up, and listed by the page search, along this path, earlier entries first:
.IP 1. 3
\fBThe project around the current directory.\fR The project root is the nearest directory upward that holds a marker: \fB\%.git\fR, \fB\%.hg\fR, \fB\%.svn\fR, \fB\%.jj\fR, \fB\%shard.yml\fR, \fB\%Cargo.toml\fR, \fB\%package.json\fR, \fB\%go.mod\fR, \fB\%pyproject.toml\fR, \fB\%setup.py\fR, \fB\%Gemfile\fR, \fB\%pom.xml\fR, \fB\%build.gradle\fR, \fB\%CMakeLists.txt\fR, \fB\%meson.build\fR, \fB\%configure.ac\fR, or a \fB\%Makefile\fR. The search stops below the home directory. Without a marker, the current directory itself is the root, unless it is the home directory or \fB\%/\fR. The root and its subfolders are searched up to 4 levels deep, skipping hidden folders, \fB\%node_modules\fR, \fB\%vendor\fR, \fB\%lib\fR, \fB\%target\fR, \fB\%__pycache__\fR and symbolic links. Two kinds of directory count: trees with section directories (\fB\%man/man1/tool.1\fR), and directories with pages in them (\fB\%doc/tool.1\fR, or the root itself). A page there is a file named \fB\%name.section\fR (optionally \fB\%.gz\fR) whose text starts with a roff control character. The Markdown documents found along the way (not hidden ones) are listed by the page search, by their path from the project root.
.IP 2. 3
\fBThe added directories\fR, from \fB\%docv \%path\-add\fR.
.IP 3. 3
\fBThe system path\fR, built as man\-db does (manpath(5)):
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%MANPATH\fR, where each empty component stands for the default path;
.IP \(bu 2
otherwise the default path: for each \fB\%PATH\fR element, its \fB\%MANPATH_MAP\fR directories from \fB\%/etc/man_db.conf\fR (or \fB\%/etc/manpath.config\fR), or else whichever of \fB\%../man\fR, \fB\%man\fR, \fB\%../share/man\fR and \fB\%share/man\fR exist next to it;
.IP \(bu 2
then the \fB\%MANDATORY_MANPATH\fR directories.
.RE
.PP
Locale subdirectories of a tree come before it (\fB\%LC_ALL\fR, \fB\%LC_MESSAGES\fR, \fB\%LANG\fR), and sections are searched in man\-db\(aqs order.
.PP
.TS
tab(	);
l lx.
T{
\fBCommand\fR
T}	T{
\fBDoes\fR
T}
_
T{
\fB\%docv \%path\-add \%dir\[u2026]\fR
T}	T{
adds directories (made absolute) to \fB\%$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/docv/paths\fR; each must exist; a directory without man pages gets a warning
T}
T{
\fB\%docv \%path\-rm \%dir\[u2026]\fR
T}	T{
removes them; fails for a directory that was not added
T}
T{
\fB\%docv \%path\-list\fR
T}	T{
prints the search path, each entry with its source (\fB\%project\fR, \fB\%added\fR, \fB\%system\fR); \fB\%(pages)\fR marks a directory with pages in it
T}
.TE
.PP
\fB\%paths\fR holds one directory per line, and lines starting with \fB\%#\fR are comments. \fB\%path\-add\fR and \fB\%path\-rm\fR rewrite the file, so comments you add by hand are not kept.
.SS
Man pages of a Crystal shard
.PP
\fB\%docv \%gen\-cr\fR generates man pages for a Crystal shard: the nearest directory from \fB\%shard\fR (default: the current directory) upward that holds a \fB\%shard.yml\fR. It runs \fB\%crystal \%docs \%\-\-format \%json\fR there and renders, into \fB\%dir\fR (default \fB\%man\fR, relative to the shard root):
.PP
.TS
tab(	);
l lx.
T{
\fBPage\fR
T}	T{
\fBFrom\fR
T}
_
T{
\fB\%man7/name.7cr\fR
T}	T{
\fB\%README.md\fR: NAME from its first sentence, its sections, then the list of types
T}
T{
\fB\%man3/Type.3cr\fR
T}	T{
each documented type: synopsis with its \fB\%require\fR, description, inheritance, related types, constants or enum members, constructors, class and instance methods, macros, source files, see also
T}
T{
\fB\%man3/name\-toplevel.3cr\fR
T}	T{
the top level methods, macros and constants, when there are any
T}
.TE
.PP
The \fB\%cr\fR suffix keeps the pages from shadowing C library pages. Being under \fB\%man/\fR in the project, they show up right away in \fB\%docv\fR\(aqs page search (\fB\%/Bar\fR) and as \fB\%docv \%Foo::Bar\fR. Doc comments are Markdown (read with markd): code spans and bold, italic, lists, code blocks, quotes and tables become roff, external links get their URL in angle brackets, and paragraphs starting with \fB\%NOTE:\fR, \fB\%TODO:\fR, \fB\%DEPRECATED:\fR and the other Crystal flags get a bold label. Generated members (\fB\%record\fR\(aqs \fB\%clone\fR and \fB\%copy_with\fR, serializer constructors, enum predicates) are left out.
.PP
A page whose content is unchanged keeps its file and date, and generated pages that are no longer produced are removed. Every page carries the comment \fB\%Generated \%by \%docv \%gen\-cr \%from \%README.md \%and \%the \%doc \%comments; \%regenerate \%instead \%of \%editing.\fR; a file in the way without it stops the run. \fB\%SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH\fR sets the date for reproducible builds.
.SS
Configuration
.PP
The configuration file is \fB\%$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/docv/config.ini\fR. When \fB\%XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is unset or not an absolute path, \fB\%\(ti/.config/docv/config.ini\fR is used. The first run writes a commented default file there. Unknown sections or keys and invalid values are reported as errors.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
[roff]
; run sy, pso, pi, open, opena, write, writec, writem, close, cf, trf, rd and \eV
unsafe = false

[viewer]
start\-wrapped = true
syntax\-highlighting = true
dock = regular
dock\-width = 36
dock\-open = false
search\-threads = 0
.EE
.RE
.PP
.TS
tab(	);
l l lx.
T{
\fBKey\fR
T}	T{
\fBDefault\fR
T}	T{
\fBMeaning\fR
T}
_
T{
\fB\%roff.unsafe\fR
T}	T{
\fB\%false\fR
T}	T{
run the requests that execute commands or access files (see roff coverage); booleans accept \fB\%true\fR/\fB\%false\fR, \fB\%yes\fR/\fB\%no\fR, \fB\%on\fR/\fB\%off\fR, \fB\%1\fR/\fB\%0\fR
T}
T{
\fB\%viewer.start\-wrapped\fR
T}	T{
\fB\%true\fR
T}	T{
start in the wrapped layout; \fB\%false\fR starts unwrapped (\fB\%w\fR switches, \fB\%\-u\fR starts unwrapped either way)
T}
T{
\fB\%viewer.syntax\-highlighting\fR
T}	T{
\fB\%true\fR
T}	T{
color code examples by their syntax (see Syntax highlighting)
T}
T{
\fB\%viewer.dock\fR
T}	T{
\fB\%regular\fR
T}	T{
\fB\%regular\fR: the page narrows to make room for the dock; \fB\%overlay\fR: the dock covers the left of the page
T}
T{
\fB\%viewer.dock\-width\fR
T}	T{
\fB\%36\fR
T}	T{
width of the dock in columns (16 to 400); dragging its right edge changes it until the viewer quits
T}
T{
\fB\%viewer.dock\-open\fR
T}	T{
\fB\%false\fR
T}	T{
open the dock at start
T}
T{
\fB\%viewer.search\-threads\fR
T}	T{
\fB\%0\fR
T}	T{
processes that read the text of every page and threads that search it (see Dock); \fB\%0\fR is one less than the number of processors
T}
.TE
.SS
Colors
.PP
The viewer draws every element in a \fIrole\fR, and \fB\%[colors]\fR in \fB\%config.ini\fR restyles roles. The default file lists every role with its default, commented out.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
[colors]
heading = bold #ff8800
field = on background+8
selection = black on bright\-cyan
.EE
.RE
.PP
A style is attribute words (\fB\%bold\fR, \fB\%italic\fR, \fB\%underline\fR, \fB\%reverse\fR, \fB\%dim\fR), a foreground color, and \fB\%on\fR followed by a background color. A color is one of:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%default\fR, \fB\%foreground\fR or \fB\%background\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
an ANSI name: \fB\%black\fR, \fB\%red\fR, \fB\%green\fR, \fB\%yellow\fR, \fB\%blue\fR, \fB\%magenta\fR, \fB\%cyan\fR, \fB\%white\fR, or \fB\%bright\-\fR followed by one of them;
.IP \(bu 2
a palette number \fB\%0\fR\-\fB\%255\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%#rrggbb\fR.
.PP
Named and numbered colors are the terminal theme\(aqs own, so the defaults fit any theme. A \fB\%+N\fR or \fB\%\-N\fR suffix ramps a color N percent toward the theme\(aqs foreground or background. For example, \fB\%on \%background+12\fR is a bar a shade off the background in a dark and a light theme alike, and \fB\%foreground\-40\fR is text dimmed toward the background. Comments go on their own lines.
.PP
On entering, the viewer asks the terminal for its theme with OSC 21 (kitty\(aqs color control protocol: foreground, background and the 16 ANSI colors), and asks again when the color scheme changes. Once the terminal answers, ramped colors are exact and written as 24\-bit color. Without an answer, xterm\(aqs colors on a black or white background (by the reported color scheme) stand in. The color depth then comes from the environment: 24\-bit with \fB\%COLORTERM=truecolor\fR or \fB\%24bit\fR or a \fB\%*\-direct\fR \fB\%TERM\fR, 256 colors with a \fB\%256color\fR \fB\%TERM\fR, the 16 ANSI colors otherwise. Colors past the depth are mapped to the nearest color the terminal shows. With \fB\%NO_COLOR\fR set (and not empty), the viewer shows no colors at all and uses attributes only: bold headings, underlined arguments and links, reverse video for selections. Colors a page sets itself with roff color requests are kept, and are dropped with \fB\%NO_COLOR\fR.
.PP
.TS
